 @Vsevolod3788  Actually no not really, while I do concur that Cyberpunk really doesn't actually look all this good and am pretty surprised for how last gen it kind of looks and how much the models basically remind me of cartoonish mid-2010s models, basically it looks just like Witcher 3 to me for the most part, but they made TEXTURES much better. This kills the nVidia GPU. Because while we had some monstrous stagnation in gaming throughout the 2010s to where a game made in 2011 was probably going to be near indistinguishable from a 2019 game, what they did do was to basically start adding much high quality textures. Which I will say, all the environmental objects like trash do look well trash, that the overall complexion of the Cyberpunk world is superbly blended to make a complete whole. I do think it's a rather lifeless city in certain sense, but only insofar as GTA V or Watchdogs or Witcher 3 or something can be called a bit lifeless, and meanwhile it seldom suffers from the bad texturework of 2010s which is where basically all the devs tried making every model look as cutscene quality as possible, but completely at the expense of ignoring the entire environment.

Idk, maybe this is just how neurotypicals view the universe, through this humanlike eye where pretty much all they see and cognitively process is human faces and so they miss out all the other details of their existence and purely as emotional beings watch for rich deeply detailed facial expressions on human and basically all neurologically wired to be stupid at literally everything else, basically human beings seeming to exist as somewhere between bydlo and facial expression reading devices, but I insist that there's more a dev can do than just focus his entire time on making the human face look visually photorealistic but then forget everything else. This makes many such world crap to be in, because there's low environmental effects, smudgy, mid-2000s looking textures everywhere, the sound needs work, Deus Ex was great example where reloading a pistol mag sound like eating a bag of chips (like wtf?) and it ultimately added up to a poorly drawn out world.

Thankfully we are exiting this mode now and so while I'd certainly have argued that in 2000s, because even the models barely improved, it's now all catching up to the near photorealism of character animations. Quantum Break for example is a really old game and Cyberpunk basically looks like that. But brand new games now a lot of them all their environments are looking pretty good, and I have AMD to thank for that. Were it not for AMD we'd still be having these muddy, barely legible textures on everything and everybody, stone walls would look like it was made to be played on the Playstation 2. Bad textures everywhere. But instead we now have 14gb worth of textures, and it's made a noticeable impact on world quality. Somehow even in a sub-8gb game like Cyberpunk, it makes the world incredibly detailed usually, with the entire environment richly coming together.

