 @mspenelopy  Well, I had to be the "well actually" guy but here it is. AMD has less driver issues than nVIDIA on the whole. What AMD does have is more severe driver issues than nVIDIA. nVIDIA have around 3-4 times more moderate issues than AMD and a heck of a lot more minor ones. You can see this by comparing their driver release notes and there is a Youtube who did just that.

So AMD drivers, on the whole, are indeed less issue prone but they are more prone to getting a severe issue. As for me, I've had no issues. Some people who bought the 5700 XT around the same time I did had Black Screen Issues (severe driver issue) but it wasn't affecting me. I also haven't had an issue with my Radeon VII, Vega 64s, Vega 56s, RX 6800 XT, RX 6700 XT, etc. I mean the only card that gives me issues is my R9 Fury-X Crossfire setup but that's because they haven't gotten a legit AMD driver update in years. I have used 3rd party drivers and those work wonderfully.

So yeah, the 5700 XT Black Screen issue was a pretty severe one to point out but it didn't affect everyone. In my book, there is no reason to buy nVIDIA at all. I mean there's Raytracing performance at the absolute high end (RTX 4090) so that's maybe the only selling point for a gamer in my book. For content creation, however, nVIDIA CUDA has no competition.

